NSTableView: push row selection through the table view's own
selectionIndexes binding.

The selectionIndexes binding is bidirectional on Apple: when the user
clicks a row, the bound NSArrayController's selection must follow.
GSKeyValueBinding keys bindings by the object that called -bind:, which
for a nib-established binding is the table view itself - but
-_postSelectionDidChangeNotification only looked for a selectionIndexes
binding registered with the observed controller as the source object
(found via the first column's value binding), so the reverse push never
fired for nib-loaded tables. The controller's selection then stays
stale, and anything driven by controller.selectedObjects (remove
buttons, detail views) operates on the wrong objects.

Check the table view's own binding first; keep the previous
controller-as-source lookup as a fallback, now guarded against an empty
column array.

Repro: Scripts/gnustep-patch-repros/tableview-selection-push-test.m
(pristine master: RESULT: FAIL, patched: RESULT: PASS).

diff --git a/Source/NSTableView.m b/Source/NSTableView.m
index 31791f9..81ab11c 100644
--- a/Source/NSTableView.m
+++ b/Source/NSTableView.m
@@ -6804,23 +6804,33 @@ For a more detailed explanation, -setSortDescriptors:. */
 
 - (void) _postSelectionDidChangeNotification
 {
-  NSTableColumn *tb = [_tableColumns objectAtIndex: 0];
   GSKeyValueBinding *theBinding;
-  
-  theBinding = [GSKeyValueBinding getBinding: NSValueBinding
-				   forObject: tb];
 
-  // If there is a binding, send the indexes back
+  theBinding = [GSKeyValueBinding getBinding: NSSelectionIndexesBinding
+				   forObject: self];
   if (theBinding != nil)
     {
-      id observedObject = [theBinding observedObject];
+      [theBinding reverseSetValue: _selectedRows];
+    }
+  else if ([_tableColumns count] > 0)
+    {
+      NSTableColumn *tb = [_tableColumns objectAtIndex: 0];
 
-      // Set the selection indexes on the controller...
-      theBinding = [GSKeyValueBinding getBinding: NSSelectionIndexesBinding
-				       forObject: observedObject];
+      theBinding = [GSKeyValueBinding getBinding: NSValueBinding
+				       forObject: tb];
+
+      // If there is a binding, send the indexes back
       if (theBinding != nil)
 	{
-	  [theBinding reverseSetValue: _selectedRows];
+	  id observedObject = [theBinding observedObject];
+
+	  // Set the selection indexes on the controller...
+	  theBinding = [GSKeyValueBinding getBinding: NSSelectionIndexesBinding
+					   forObject: observedObject];
+	  if (theBinding != nil)
+	    {
+	      [theBinding reverseSetValue: _selectedRows];
+	    }
 	}
     }
 
